@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Workspace dependency trees — one table, isolated by copy, installed before the agent

' covers: packages/lib/entrypoint-lib.sh, internal/workspace/deps.go,
'         internal/workspace/mount.go, internal/run/run.go,
'         defs/opencode/entrypoint.sh, defs/cursor/entrypoint.sh,
'         defs/cecli/entrypoint.sh, defs/claudecode/mcp/entrypoint.sh
' covers: internal/workspace/platform.go
'
' ── WHAT THE IN-CONTAINER STEP DOES, IN ORDER, PER PROJECT ─────────────────
'   1. a HOST-BUILT tree is made usable. On the docker backend proveo never
'      binds a dependency directory from the host: each one the table names is
'      a PRIVATE COPY, reused on the off chance host and container OS/arch
'      agree. When they do not — the probe finds a non-ELF object — the copy is
'      cleared and rebuilt for this platform, and the operator's checkout is
'      never touched. On sbx the tree IS the host's (the checkout is mirrored
'      at its own path), so rebuilding in place is opt-in (PROVEO_DEPS=
'      reinstall) and the remedy on offer is --clone.
'   2. the INSTALL runs before the agent does. A workspace with nothing
'      installed is the single most confusing state to hand an agent — every
'      import fails and every tool guesses why — so an absent tree is
'      installed and a present one refreshed against its lockfile by the
'      managers for which that is a cheap no-op. Lockfiles are RESPECTED
'      (--frozen-lockfile, --immutable, ci) and never rewritten: a drifted
'      lockfile is reported, because rewriting it is the agent's task if it is
'      anyone's.
'
' Every language has a row even when the row is "nothing", because a missing
' row is indistinguishable from an oversight. Languages that install nothing
' each have a stated reason: python's environment is built by
' ensure_python_env; cpp resolves through system or external package managers
' proveo does not drive; zig fetches at build time; java/kotlin resolution
' (gradle/maven) is heavy and the build performs it; nix realises its closure
' on demand.
'
' _dep_install_idempotent says whether re-running an install on a tree that is
' already present and native is a cheap no-op. `npm ci` is THE exception: it
' deletes node_modules first, every time, so it runs only when the tree is
' absent or foreign.
'
' _proveo_dep_is_isolated reports whether a directory is proveo's private copy
' rather than the host's tree: a copy is bind-mounted at exactly that path, so
' it is its own mount point, while the host's tree sits INSIDE the workspace
' mount and is not. /proc is the source of truth on the docker backend, and
' anywhere it is unreadable the answer is "not isolated" — the conservative one.
'
' A pnpm/npm/yarn/bun WORKSPACE installs every member from its root, hoisting
' into one node_modules, so a member with no node_modules of its own is the
' normal state rather than "nothing is installed" — and `npm ci` inside a
' member, which has no lockfile, is an error. Members collapse into their root.
'
' ── BUN IS PINNED THROUGH mise, NOT corepack ───────────────────────────────
' The floor ships one bun (defs/base-node); a project asks for its own with
' `packageManager: "bun@1.2.3"` (an exact pin — bun's own reading of the
' field), `engines.bun` (a range), or a `.bun-version` file. A bun lockfile
' alone means "use the bun that is here", never "reinstall". corepack does not
' manage bun, so the pin is honoured through mise, which carries bun as a core
' tool — the same path engines.node takes when the running node fails its range.
'
' ── WHY EVERY ROW IS HOST-BUILT CONTENT ────────────────────────────────────
' ── THE TABLE LIVES TWICE, AND THE LOCKSTEP IS A TEST ──────────────────────
' internal/workspace.DepLangs decides which host directories the mount plan
' hides behind a private copy; the shell's _dep_lang_markers/_dep_lang_dirs
' decide which trees the seed then installs into. They must name the same
' directories for the same projects, or a tree gets isolated but never
' installed (an empty overlay the agent trips on), or installed but never
' isolated (a Linux install written into the operator's macOS checkout).
' internal/contract/deps_table_test.go is that lockstep.
'
' ── WHAT THE WORKSPACE BIND WOULD OTHERWISE CARRY ──────────────────────────
' Go's vendor/ is checked-in module code and portable, so it is mounted as
' SOURCE. The host-built tree that can live under it — ruby's vendor/bundle —
' is a DepLangs row and gets its own overlay on top of that bind. Root
' dependency trees (the hoisted node_modules a workspace member resolves
' through) arrive as plain copies too, gated by MountRootDeps
' (PROVEO_MOUNT_ROOT_DEPS=0 turns it off); trees under the scope itself are
' isolated regardless.
'
' Every directory the table names holds platform binaries (.node, .so,
' .bundle, provider executables), a venv's interpreter, or a toolchain's build
' output. None of it may cross the workspace bind as-is, because the container
' cannot execute what the host compiled — and worse, the PORTABLE MAJORITY of
' each tree loads fine, so the failure surfaces late, at the first call into a
' native module, naming the tool rather than the platform.
'
' The table is language-uniform on purpose. node_modules is only the loudest
' instance; a fix shaped like the one ecosystem that broke is how the next
' ecosystem breaks the same way. It stays in lockstep with _dep_lang_markers /
' _dep_lang_dirs in packages/lib/entrypoint-lib.sh, and internal/contract pins
' the two together so editing one without the other fails the build.
'
' Languages whose tooling leaves nothing host-specific in-tree (go, java,
' kotlin, nix — modules resolve outside the workspace or to portable bytecode)
' have no row in DepLangs, but they DO have an explicit row in the shell's
' _dep_lang_class, where "nothing to isolate" is a recorded decision rather
' than an absence.
'
' A tree's Host path may not exist: a checkout with nothing installed still
' gets an overlay, so the install the seed runs lands in the copy and never in
' the host tree. Dep dirs themselves and the shared prune set are never
' descended into — a nested node_modules inside node_modules is the package
' manager's business.
'
' ── THE TWO LAYOUTS, AND WHY THEY DIFFER ───────────────────────────────────
'   under the scope tree (bind-mounted at /app or /app/<rel>) every table row
'   found by the walk gets an overlay, present or not: the parent bind would
'   otherwise carry the host tree in, or carry a container install out — the
'   ping-pong this exists to stop.
'   at the repo root of a subdir scope only trees that EXIST are copied (gated
'   by MountRootDeps, yielding to a scope dir of the same name), because /app
'   itself is container filesystem there — a hoisted install the agent runs
'   lands in the container without any help.
'
' ── PURE PLAN, WRITING STEP ────────────────────────────────────────────────
' DepCopies reads the filesystem the way Plan does (existence, markers) and
' writes nothing; MaterializeDeps is the one part that writes, which is why it
' is a separate step. Plan stays pure, and --print never copies a
' multi-gigabyte tree to render an argv. Materialising is best-effort per tree:
' a copy that fails still leaves an empty directory, which isolates and
' installs, and both the copies made and the joined errors come back so the
' caller can report both.
'
' copyTree asks for clonefile(2) first on macOS: on APFS that is
' copy-on-write, so a multi-gigabyte node_modules stages in milliseconds and
' costs no disk until the container writes to it. cp falls back to a byte copy
' on its own when cloning is impossible (a different volume, a non-APFS
' filesystem).
'
' The staging root is the run's DepStage under the per-run state dir so
' `proveo clean` can reclaim it. The fallback keeps a caller that never set it
' isolated anyway, keyed by pid so concurrent runs cannot collide and repeated
' Plan calls in one process agree.
'
' ── WHY THE POLICY ASKS ABOUT PLATFORM, AND WHERE IT READS IT ──────────────
' The defs build linux images only — defs/lib/docker-build.sh publishes
' linux/amd64 and linux/arm64, and a local load builds linux/<host arch> — and
' the runner passes no --platform, so docker picks the daemon default: the host
' architecture unless DOCKER_DEFAULT_PLATFORM says otherwise. ImagePlatform
' reproduces exactly that rule, so the decision matches what docker will do
' without asking the daemon — which --print must not.
'
'   equal (a Linux host of the image's arch)  the tree runs as-is, so the copy
'     saves the install entirely and the seed's probe confirms it native.
'   different (macOS, Windows, cross-arch)    every native module in the tree
'     is foreign, the seed would clear the copy and reinstall anyway — so the
'     copy is skipped and the install IS the plan.
'
' PROVEO_DEPS_COPY overrides: `always` copies regardless (a pure-JS tree from a
' macOS host loads fine and spares a registry round-trip under a tight egress
' tier); `never` skips even on a match. The reason is returned for the operator.
'
' ── WHY sbx STRIPS THEM ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' StripDepCopies exists because the sbx backend mounts every path at its own
' HOST path and has no way to say "this directory, at that container path", so
' a nested overlay has no expression there. Passed through, each copy would
' become an unrelated extra workspace while the host tree still rode in inside
' the mirrored checkout. On sbx the only thing that keeps host-built trees out
' is --clone, and the caller says so when it drops any.

node "Host checkout" as HOST <<host>> {
  database "source\nbind-mounted at /app" as SRC <<db>>
  database "dependency trees\n""node_modules"" · "".venv"" · ""vendor/bundle""\n""target"" · "".terraform"" · ""lua_modules""" as DEP <<error>>
}

node "proveo run — host side" as RUN <<host>> {
  component "**table** — ""workspace.DepLangs""\nmarkers → dirs, one row per language" as TABLE <<app>>
  component "**plan** — ""MountSpec.DepCopies""\nwalk to depth 4 · one copy per tree, present or not" as PLAN <<app>>
  component "**decide** — ""DepCopyPolicy""\nhost ""GOOS/GOARCH"" vs image ""linux/<arch>""" as POLICY <<app>>
  component "**stage** — ""MaterializeDeps""\ncopy on match · empty on mismatch\n""<state>/egress/<sid>/deps/""" as STAGE <<app>>
}

node "Container — ""proveo_seed"" → ""ensure_dependency_trees""" as C <<host>> {
  component "**discover** — ""_proveo_project_roots""\nsame markers, same depth" as SCAN <<app>>
  component "**classify** — ""_dep_lang_class""\naddons · artifacts · provisioned · portable · none" as CLASS <<app>>
  component "**probe** — ""_proveo_foreign_object""\nELF magic, capped scan" as PROBE <<app>>
  component "**rebuild** — ""_dep_clear_tree"" + ""_dep_install""\nonly where ""_proveo_dep_is_isolated""" as FIX <<app>>
  component "**install** — ""_dep_install""\nabsent → install · native → refresh" as INST <<app>>
}

cloud "Package registry" as REG <<cloud>>
component "Agent" as AGENT <<app>>

TABLE ARROW_MAIN PLAN
PLAN  ARROW_MAIN STAGE : ""DepCopy{Host, Stage, Container}""
POLICY ARROW_MAIN STAGE : reuse? · ""PROVEO_DEPS_COPY"" overrides
DEP   ARROW_OPTIONAL STAGE : plain copy — only when platforms match
STAGE ARROW_MAIN C : mounted rw at ""/app/…/<dir>""
DEP   ARROW_ERROR SRC : never bind-mounted (docker)
SCAN  ARROW_MAIN CLASS
CLASS ARROW_MAIN PROBE : addons · artifacts
PROBE ARROW_MAIN FIX : any non-ELF object
FIX   ARROW_MAIN INST
CLASS ARROW_MAIN INST : portable · absent addons
INST  ARROW_CLOUD REG : lockfile-respecting fetch
INST  ARROW_MAIN AGENT : exec — dependencies in place

note bottom of DEP
  **Not a TypeScript hazard.** ""node_modules"" is the
  loudest instance: gems, luarocks and terraform
  providers all carry host binaries, a venv carries
  the host's interpreter, and build output is stale.
  The remedy is a table, never a special case.
end note

note right of TABLE
  **Two tables, pinned together.** ""DepLangs"" decides
  what the plan hides; ""_dep_lang_dirs"" decides what
  the seed installs into. ""internal/contract"" fails
  the build when they disagree — a tree isolated but
  never installed is an empty overlay the agent trips on.
end note

note bottom of PLAN
  **Pure.** ""Plan"" only NAMES the copies, keyed by
  container path, so ""--print"" renders the real argv
  without copying a tree. ""stageDeps"" (""internal/run"")
  materialises after the backend is chosen and
  removes the stage when the run ends.
end note

note bottom of STAGE
  **Absent on the host is still an overlay.** An empty
  staged dir binds over the would-be path, so the
  install the seed runs lands in the copy — not in a
  Linux ""node_modules"" written into a macOS checkout.
  At a subdir scope's repo root only present trees copy.
end note

note right of POLICY
  **Copy only what can run here.** The defs build
  ""linux/<arch>"" and the runner passes no ""--platform"",
  so the image is linux at the host arch (or
  ""DOCKER_DEFAULT_PLATFORM""). A macOS tree is foreign in
  every native module — the seed would clear the copy —
  so a mismatch stages empty and the install is the plan.
end note

note right of FIX
  **A copy may be cleared; the host's tree may not.**
  ""/proc/self/mountinfo"" tells them apart: the copy is
  its own mount point. A package manager keeps the
  foreign binaries it finds, so a rebuild starts empty.
  Host tree: ""PROVEO_DEPS=reinstall"" or ""--clone"".
end note

note right of INST
  **Install first, then the agent.** Absent addons are
  installed; a native tree is refreshed only by managers
  where that is a cheap no-op (""npm ci"" wipes — absent
  or foreign only). A workspace root installs once for
  every member. Lockfiles are respected, never rewritten.
end note

note bottom of REG
  **sbx cannot overlay.** It mirrors the checkout at its
  host path; ""StripDepCopies"" drops the staged dirs and
  the run warns that host trees cross. Clone mode — the
  sbx default — carries tracked files only, so nothing
  host-built arrives and the seed installs into the clone.
end note
@enduml
